sections.body:
In modern React, a **component** is, at its simplest, just a JavaScript function that accepts a single argument -- an object of **props** -- and returns a description of what should appear on screen (a React element, typically written using JSX). That's the entire mental model: a component is a function; calling it (which React does for you) produces a description of UI, not a direct DOM mutation.

```jsx
function Greeting(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;
}
```

Components compose the same way regular functions do -- a component can render other components inside its own returned JSX, and a whole application ends up being a tree of components calling other components, mirroring a call tree. One firm naming convention matters here: component names must start with a capital letter (`Greeting`, not `greeting`) -- this is how JSX itself tells apart `<Greeting />` (treated as a reference to your component function) from `<div>` or `<span>` (treated as a literal, built-in HTML tag).

JSX looks deliberately similar to HTML, which is exactly what makes it easy to misread as HTML that somehow got embedded inside JavaScript. It is not that. JSX is a syntax extension that a build tool (typically Babel) compiles down to ordinary JavaScript **before** it ever reaches the browser -- specifically, into nested calls to `React.createElement(type, props, ...children)`.

```jsx
const element = <h1 className="title">Hello, {name}!</h1>;

// The line above compiles to something equivalent to:
const element = React.createElement(
  "h1",
  { className: "title" },
  "Hello, ",
  name,
  "!"
);
```

Because JSX is really just JavaScript function calls in disguise, several of its rules follow directly from that fact rather than being arbitrary syntax choices: attributes use `{ }` to embed any JavaScript *expression* (`className={isActive ? "active" : ""}`), not just plain string literals; attribute names are camelCase (`className`, `onClick`) because they're really just JavaScript object property names being passed to `createElement`, and `class` and standard DOM event names cannot be reused verbatim (`class` is a reserved word in JavaScript); tags without children must be self-closed (`<img />`, not `<img>`), since JSX has no separate 'this tag never has children' notion the way HTML does; and a component's `return` can only produce a single root element (or a `Fragment`, `<>...</>`), because a JavaScript function can only return one value, and `React.createElement` calls nest the same way any other function call would. Because none of this is understood natively by browsers, JSX always requires a compile/build step -- it never runs as-is.

examples.code:
// IMPERATIVE: you manage every DOM update by hand
function renderImperativeCounter(container, count) {
  container.innerHTML = "";
  const button = document.createElement("button");
  button.textContent = `Clicked ${count} times`;
  button.onclick = () => renderImperativeCounter(container, count + 1);
  container.appendChild(button);
}

// DECLARATIVE: describe what the button should look like for a given count
// React handles finding and updating the real DOM node for you
function Counter({ count, onIncrement }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onIncrement}>
      Clicked {count} times
    </button>
  );
}

const name = "Amara";
const element = <h1 className="title">Hello, {name}!</h1>;

// The JSX above is syntactic sugar for this ordinary JavaScript call:
const sameElement = React.createElement(
  "h1",
  { className: "title" },
  "Hello, ",
  name,
  "!"
);

function Greeting({ name, isNewUser }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Hello, {name}!</h2>
      {isNewUser && <p>Welcome -- thanks for joining.</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage from a parent component:
<Greeting name="Amara" isNewUser={true} />
<Greeting name="Ben" isNewUser={false} />

